{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler of the County of Tripoli during the 12th century a period of significant political and military activity in the Levant. He reigned from 1184 to 1202 leaving a lasting impact on the region's power dynamics and its relationship with the Crusader states. His reign is characterized by military campaigns political maneuvering and a consolidation of Tripoli's position as a major maritime power.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Eleanor of Villanova. He inherited the county at a young age following his father's death in 1184. His early years were marked by internal conflicts and challenges to his authority. He faced opposition from rival factions within Tripoli and struggled to maintain control over the county's territory. He quickly demonstrated his military acumen and political shrewdness establishing himself as a capable leader. He focused on strengthening Tripoli's defenses and expanding its influence in the Mediterranean.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw significant military achievements. He successfully defended Tripoli from numerous attacks by Muslim forces and expanded the county's territory through successful campaigns. He established a strong military presence and built a formidable army capable of challenging the power of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum. His military victories solidified Tripoli's position as a major player in the Levant. He also fostered trade and economic growth promoting Tripoli as a vital link between Europe and the East.\n\n*Political and Military Campaigns* Raymond III engaged in numerous political and military campaigns throughout his reign. He frequently clashed with the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum and other Muslim powers seeking to maintain Tripoli's independence. He also forged alliances with other Crusader states including the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Edessa. These alliances proved crucial in defending Tripoli from external threats. Raymond also actively engaged in diplomatic efforts seeking to secure favorable trade agreements and maintain peaceful relations with neighboring territories. His military campaigns were often costly but ultimately successful in preserving Tripoli's sovereignty.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1202 at the age of 58. His death marked the end of an era in Tripoli's history. He left behind a stable and prosperous county that had successfully defended itself against numerous enemies. His reign is remembered as a period of strength and resilience. His military victories and political achievements ensured Tripoli's continued existence as a major power in the Levant for many years to come. He is regarded as one of the most successful and influential rulers of the County of Tripoli. His legacy continues to be studied by historians who seek to understand the political and military landscape of the medieval Levant.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.14342188835144043}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -4.5060096454108134e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.0798519179224968}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.04111293703317642}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5501455068588257}, {"token": "▁ruler", "logprob": -0.6038829684257507}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -0.8661623597145081}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.00040236959466710687}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.07524044811725616}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -1.883488948806189e-05}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "▁during", "logprob": -0.2162804901599884}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0037934263236820698}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.16847649216651917}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.7683704451628